# Fingerprints of confirmed false positives, in gitleaks' own format:
# <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# Each one is a fabricated value inside a test that proves log redaction
# works - the string has to look enough like a secret to trigger the same
# detector redaction defends against, or the test proves nothing. See
# docs/security/POLICIES.md for the redaction rules these strings exercise.

# app/tests/test_asvs_l2_closure.py::test_a_labelled_secret_in_a_message_is_redacted
# One of the parametrized messages in that test reads as a labelled token to
# gitleaks by design - it is what the test asserts gets redacted. Not
# reproduced here, deliberately: quoting the flagged text in this comment
# would just make gitleaks flag this file too (it did, on the first attempt).
b4de77d550281967fd7d013906867a257029c43d:app/tests/test_asvs_l2_closure.py:generic-api-key:208

# This file's own history: an earlier commit quoted that same fixture text
# in the comment above before it was reworded to stop doing that. gitleaks
# scans full history, not just the current tree, so the fingerprint of the
# now-fixed commit needs suppressing too rather than only the live line.
7afe57899e0768b3388ee8d78819452d5762aacb:.gitleaksignore:generic-api-key:10
